Jakob Creutzig <creutzig@mathematik.tu-darmstadt.de> writes:

> Speaking of realism, ever tried to mount a horse while in 
> a platemail?

No I haven't. But all medieval and reneissance history books that have
been written after 1900 that I've read agree that knights did *not*
have serious troubles in mounting.

Also, when the unknown contemporary biographer of Marshal Boucicaut,
Jean le Maingre (1366-1421) praised the martial prowess of his
subject, one of his examples was that Boucicaut could jump to his
saddle from ground while in full armor. (See:
http://members.cox.net/illusionarmoring/14th.html for an armor of that
time period). (Other examples include making chin-ups and climbing
underside of ladders in full armor). 

And BTW, the term "plate mail" is an idiotism coined up by 19th
century English historians that was unfortunately adopted into RPGs.
It is idiocy since "mail" means a "net" and in the Medieval times it
was used exclusively for the armor that is nowadays too often known as
"chain mail". The contemporaries used mostly "harness" (or any of its
variant spellings) to denote the plate armor. 

> AFAIK it was common to lift them in the saddle with a crane.

No, it wasn't. The concept of "knight cranes" appears to be invention
of Mark Twain. 

Though, in the later periods (mid-15th century onwards) jousting armor
was much heavier than combat armor. It was not uncommon to have two or
even three heavy breastplates protecting the left side of the body
(the right side was left with one layer as hits to it were not as
common or dangerous as those to the left side). The fastest way to
differentiate a jousting armor from combat armor is to look at the
helm: if it is bolted to the breastplate, then it is for jousting.
(This also means that a jousting knight could not turn his head).


